# Engineering student note: trinetra.buildfile.fragment handles deployment, startup, configuration, and target integration.
# Comments are kept simple so the build/deployment steps are easy to trace.
# TRINETRA QNX 8.0 boot integration fragment
#
# This is a buildfile/IFS fragment, not a shell script. Add it to the Raspberry
# Pi BSP buildfile only after the six binaries and required libraries are part
# of the image. The commands execute after procnto starts the boot script.
#
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_ALERT=/path/to/TRINETRA_ALERT
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_WARNING_GOVERNOR=/path/to/TRINETRA_WARNING_GOVERNOR
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_HAZARD_FUSION=/path/to/TRINETRA_HAZARD_FUSION
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_VALIDATOR=/path/to/TRINETRA_VALIDATOR
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_SENSOR_INGEST=/path/to/TRINETRA_SENSOR_INGEST
# [type=link] /bin/TRINETRA_SENSOR_SIM=/path/to/TRINETRA_SENSOR_SIM
#
# Then, in the [+script] section, launch downstream to upstream:
# [pri=40f] /bin/TRINETRA_ALERT &
# [pri=35f] /bin/TRINETRA_WARNING_GOVERNOR &
# [pri=30f] /bin/TRINETRA_HAZARD_FUSION &
# [pri=25f] /bin/TRINETRA_VALIDATOR &
# [pri=20f] /bin/TRINETRA_SENSOR_INGEST &
# [pri=10f] /bin/TRINETRA_SENSOR_SIM &
#
# IMPORTANT:
# 1. Replace the example source paths with the actual BSP build paths.
# 2. Do not add the simulator to a production image unless the test scenario
#    is intentionally required at boot.
# 3. Verify that the target security policy permits these applications.
